/* data/learn-c/chem-12-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-08"] = {"8.1":{"s":"Transition (d-block) elements sit between the s and p blocks, and their properties change gradually across. Inner transition (f-block) elements, the lanthanoids and actinoids, sit at the bottom of the periodic table. This chapter covers both families and some important compounds.","x":["The d-block elements sit in the middle of the periodic table, between the s-block on the left and the p-block on the right. In their atoms, the last electron goes into a d orbital of the inner shell, (n−1)d. They are all metals, and most are hard and shiny with high melting points. Iron, copper, gold, silver, zinc and mercury are all d-block elements.","The f-block elements, or inner transition elements, have the last electron in the 4f or 5f orbitals, two shells inside. They are placed at the bottom of the table as two rows of 14 elements: the lanthanoids and the actinoids.","This chapter looks at the 3d series (Sc to Zn) in detail: configuration, oxidation states, colour, magnetism, catalytic activity, and the important compounds KMnO₄ and K₂Cr₂O₇. It then covers the extraction of metals and the f-block elements."],"e":"Iron is used for bridges and machines because it is strong. Copper is used for wires since it conducts well. Both are d-block metals."},"8.2":{"s":"Transition elements are in groups 3 to 12 and periods 4 to 7, forming the 3d, 4d, 5d and 6d series. A transition element has a partly filled d subshell in the atom or in a common ion. Zinc, cadmium and mercury are not regarded as transition elements, because they have a full d¹⁰ shell in the atom and in the +2 ion. Copper counts, because Cu(II) is 3d⁹.","x":["The d-block lies in groups 3 to 12 and in periods 4 to 7. There are four series: the 3d series (Sc to Zn, period 4), the 4d series (Y to Cd, period 5), the 5d series (La, then Hf to Hg, period 6) and the 6d series (Ac, then Rf onwards, period 7, which is incomplete).","Strictly, a transition element is one that has a partly filled d subshell in the atom or in any of its common ions. Zinc, cadmium and mercury have d¹⁰ in the atom and in the +2 ion, so they are not true transition elements, but they are studied with the d-block as their properties are similar.","The f-block elements (lanthanoids, Z = 58–71, and actinoids, Z = 90–103) are placed below the main table to keep it compact. They are inner transition elements."],"e":"Scandium (Sc) has the d¹ configuration in the atom, and its ion Sc³⁺ is d⁰. Whether it counts as a transition element depends on the strict definition, though most books include it."},"8.3":{"s":"The general configuration is (n−1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns⁰⁻². The 3d series runs from Sc (Z = 21) to Zn (Z = 30). Chromium (3d⁵4s¹) and copper (3d¹⁰4s¹) are exceptions, because half-filled and full d subshells are extra stable.","w":["Chromium is 3d⁵4s¹ and copper 3d¹⁰4s¹, not 3d⁴4s² and 3d⁹4s².","When a transition metal forms an ion, the 4s electrons leave before the 3d electrons."],"x":["The general configuration of d-block atoms is (n−1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns⁰⁻². For the 3d series it is [Ar]3d¹⁻¹⁰ 4s¹⁻². The 4s orbital fills before 3d in the atom, but once the 3d orbitals are occupied they fall below 4s in energy, which is why the 4s electrons are the first to be lost in forming ions.","The configurations: Sc 3d¹ 4s², Ti 3d² 4s², V 3d³ 4s², Cr 3d⁵ 4s¹ (exception), Mn 3d⁵ 4s², Fe 3d⁶ 4s², Co 3d⁷ 4s², Ni 3d⁸ 4s², Cu 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (exception), Zn 3d¹⁰ 4s².","The exceptions of Cr and Cu come from the extra stability of half-filled (d⁵) and fully filled (d¹⁰) subshells, which comes from a symmetrical charge distribution and a larger exchange energy. One 4s electron moves into the 3d subshell.","To write the configuration of an ion, remove the ns electrons first, then the (n−1)d electrons. Fe²⁺ is [Ar]3d⁶, Fe³⁺ is [Ar]3d⁵, Cu⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰, and Cu²⁺ is [Ar]3d⁹."],"e":"Fe: [Ar]3d⁶ 4s². Remove two 4s electrons to get Fe²⁺ [Ar]3d⁶, and one more 3d electron to get Fe³⁺ [Ar]3d⁵."},"8.4":{"s":"Transition elements show many oxidation states, because the (n−1)d and ns electrons have similar energies and can both be lost. Manganese goes from +2 up to +7. The most states appear in the middle of the series. Higher states are found in oxides and fluorides.","w":["Scandium has only one unpaired electron and shows few oxidation states; manganese shows the most.","Manganese shows the widest range of oxidation states, +2 to +7."],"x":["Transition metals show several oxidation states, because the ns and (n−1)d electrons have nearly the same energy, so after the ns electrons are used, d electrons can be used too. The states usually differ by one unit, such as Fe(+2, +3) or Mn(+2 to +7), unlike p-block elements, where they differ by two.","The most common state of the 3d metals is +2 (loss of the two 4s electrons), except for Sc (+3). The maximum state rises across the series up to Mn (+7, in MnO₄⁻), when all the electrons outside the argon core are used, and then falls: Fe up to +6, Co and Ni +4, Cu +2, Zn +2 only.","Higher oxidation states are found in oxides and fluorides (oxo-anions such as MnO₄⁻ and CrO₄²⁻). In going down a group, the higher states become more stable: for example, +6 is more stable for Mo and W than for Cr, and compounds of Cr(VI) oxidise easily while those of Mo(VI) and W(VI) do not.","The stability of an oxidation state is linked to the electronic configuration: d⁵ (Mn²⁺, Fe³⁺) and d¹⁰ (Zn²⁺) are stable. So Fe³⁺ is more stable than Fe²⁺, while Mn²⁺ is more stable than Mn³⁺. This also explains why Cr²⁺ is reducing and Mn³⁺ is oxidising."],"e":"Manganese shows +2 (MnSO₄), +3, +4 (MnO₂), +6 (K₂MnO₄) and +7 (KMnO₄), the widest range in the series."},"8.5":{"s":"All transition elements are metals. Except zinc, cadmium and mercury, which are softer and more volatile (mercury is a liquid), they are hard, shiny, malleable, ductile and good conductors. Most have high melting and boiling points, because unpaired d electrons give strong metallic bonding. They form alloys easily.","x":["All the transition elements are metals, with the typical lustre, good conduction of heat and electricity, malleability and ductility. Except for Zn, Cd, Hg and Mn, they are hard, with high melting and boiling points, since both the ns and (n−1)d electrons take part in strong metallic bonding. The melting point rises to a maximum near the middle of a series (around d⁵), where the number of unpaired electrons is largest, and then falls. Tungsten has the highest melting point of all metals, about 3400 °C.","Zinc, cadmium and mercury have low melting points (mercury is a liquid) because their d orbitals are full and do not contribute to the bonding. The densities are high, since the atoms are small and packed close, with osmium and iridium the densest of all elements, about 22.6 g/cm³.","Enthalpy of atomisation is high, and it is the energy needed to make the gaseous atoms. It helps to explain why the metals are not very reactive: they have a high energy of atomisation, though this is compensated by large hydration or lattice energies in compounds.","Many transition metals have the ability to form alloys, and they have a high density and a hard, strong structure. These properties make iron, chromium, nickel and titanium the base of many alloys."],"e":"Mercury (d¹⁰ s²) is a liquid at room temperature, while tungsten melts at about 3400 °C, which is why tungsten is used for lamp filaments."},"8.6":{"s":"Across the 3d series, atomic radius falls at first and then stays nearly the same. Ionisation enthalpy rises slowly. Many compounds are coloured (d–d transitions) and paramagnetic (unpaired electrons, with spin-only moment √(n(n+2)) BM). Transition metals form complexes and interstitial compounds, and act as catalysts.","f":[{"x":"mu = sqrt(n*(n + 2)) BM","n":"Spin-only magnetic moment in Bohr magnetons; n = number of unpaired electrons. One unpaired electron gives 1.73 BM.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 8.6, p.171"}],"w":["Count unpaired electrons first: Zn²⁺, Cu⁺ and Sc³⁺ have none, so they are diamagnetic and colourless.","The spin-only formula ignores orbital motion, so measured values differ a little."],"x":["Atomic and ionic radii. Across the 3d series, the atomic radius first decreases (as the nuclear charge increases), then stays nearly constant in the middle (Mn to Cu), and increases slightly at the end (Zn). The extra electrons go into the inner 3d subshell and shield the outer 4s electrons from the extra nuclear charge, so the decrease is small. Down a group, the radius increases from 3d to 4d, but the 4d and 5d elements have almost the same size, because of the lanthanoid contraction.","Ionisation enthalpy. The first ionisation energy rises slowly across the series, since the effective nuclear charge rises, but the increase is small as the added d electrons shield the nucleus. Zn has a higher value, as it has the full 3d¹⁰ 4s² configuration. The second ionisation energy is large for Cr and Cu, because removing an electron means breaking a stable half-filled or fully filled d subshell.","Standard electrode potentials. The E° values for M²⁺/M are mostly negative, with Cu positive (+0.34 V). The trend is irregular but roughly shows a steady fall in reducing power from Ti to Cu, as the sum of the enthalpy of atomisation, ionisation energy and hydration enthalpy changes. The value for Mn²⁺/Mn (−1.18 V) and Zn²⁺/Zn (−0.76 V) are more negative than expected, because of the stable d⁵ and d¹⁰ configurations."],"e":"Copper is the only 3d metal with a positive E°(M²⁺/M) = +0.34 V, so it does not displace hydrogen from dilute acids."},"8.7":{"s":"Two key compounds. Potassium permanganate, KMnO₄, is made from pyrolusite (MnO₂) by fusing with KOH and then oxidising the manganate. It is a strong oxidising agent, and its action changes with acidic, neutral or basic medium. Potassium dichromate, K₂Cr₂O₇, is made from chromite ore. It is an oxidising agent, and chromate and dichromate change into each other with pH.","w":["KMnO₄ is purple. In acidic solution it is reduced to almost colourless (very pale pink) Mn²⁺; in neutral solution it gives brown MnO₂.","Dichromate is orange and chromate yellow; they change into each other when you change the pH."],"x":["Potassium dichromate, K₂Cr₂O₇, is made from chromite ore, FeCr₂O₄. The ore is fused with sodium carbonate in air to give yellow sodium chromate (4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂), which is acidified to orange sodium dichromate (2Na₂CrO₄ + 2H⁺ → Na₂Cr₂O₇ + 2Na⁺ + H₂O). Treating it with KCl gives the less soluble K₂Cr₂O₇, which crystallises.","The chromate and dichromate ions are in equilibrium and depend on pH: 2CrO₄²⁻ (yellow) + 2H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange) + H₂O. Acid gives orange, and alkali gives yellow. The oxidation state of chromium stays +6.","K₂Cr₂O₇ is a strong oxidising agent in acid: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. It oxidises Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, iodide to iodine, sulphide to sulphur and alcohol to aldehyde and acid. It is a primary standard in titrations. Its equivalent mass is 294/6 = 49.","Potassium permanganate, KMnO₄, is made from pyrolusite, MnO₂. Fusion with KOH in air gives green K₂MnO₄, which is oxidised to purple KMnO₄, either electrolytically or with chlorine or ozone. In acid it is a strong oxidising agent: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (purple to colourless), with equivalent mass 158/5 = 31.6. It oxidises Fe²⁺, oxalate, H₂O₂ and sulphite. In neutral or alkaline solution it is reduced to MnO₂ (brown). It is used as a disinfectant and in titrations, and acts as its own indicator."],"e":"5Fe²⁺ + MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ → 5Fe³⁺ + Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. 20 mL of 0.02 M KMnO₄ oxidises 2.0 mmol of Fe²⁺."},"8.8":{"s":"Common features of d-block elements: variable oxidation states, coloured ions, paramagnetism, catalytic activity, complex formation, alloy formation and interstitial compounds. Most of these come from partly filled d orbitals.","x":["Here is a summary of the properties common to d-block elements. (1) Variable oxidation states, because of the nearness of the ns and (n−1)d energies. (2) Coloured ions, due to d–d transitions of electrons in the split d orbitals. d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions are colourless.","(3) Paramagnetism, since many ions have unpaired d electrons. The spin-only magnetic moment is μ = √(n(n + 2)) Bohr magnetons, for n unpaired electrons. (4) Catalytic activity, as the metals can change their oxidation state, and provide surfaces for reactions: Fe in the Haber process, V₂O₅ in the contact process, Ni in hydrogenation, Pt and Pd in many reactions.","(5) Complex formation, because of the small size and high charge of the ions, and the presence of empty d orbitals that can accept electron pairs. (6) Interstitial compounds: small atoms such as H, B, C and N fit in the gaps of the lattice. They are hard, with high melting points and keep metallic conductivity, and have no fixed formula, as in TiC, Fe₃H and Mn₄N.","(7) Alloy formation: the atoms have similar radii and can replace each other in the lattice, giving brass (Cu–Zn), bronze (Cu–Sn), steel (Fe–C) and stainless steel (Fe–Cr–Ni)."],"e":"Cu²⁺ (d⁹) is blue and paramagnetic (1 unpaired electron, 1.73 BM). Cu⁺ (d¹⁰) is colourless and diamagnetic."},"8.9":{"s":"Metallurgy is the commercial extraction of a metal from its ore. The book names pyrometallurgy, hydrometallurgy and electrometallurgy. The steps are concentration of the ore, roasting or calcination to the oxide, reduction to the metal, and refining. Iron is extracted from haematite in a blast furnace, with coke as the reducing agent. The book describes the extraction of iron in detail.","x":["Metallurgy is the process of extracting a metal from its ore, in the following steps. Concentration (dressing) removes the gangue (rock and soil) from the ore, by hand picking, washing in water, froth flotation (for sulphide ores) or magnetic separation.","Conversion to oxide: calcination, heating in the absence of air, drives off water and CO₂ (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). Roasting, heating in excess of air, converts sulphides to oxides and drives off sulphur as SO₂ (2ZnS + 3O₂ → 2ZnO + 2SO₂). Then reduction of the oxide to the metal with carbon, carbon monoxide, hydrogen, a reactive metal (aluminium in the thermite process), or electrolysis for very reactive metals.","Refining purifies the crude metal: distillation (Zn, Hg), liquation (Sn, Pb), electrolytic refining (Cu, Ag, Au: the impure metal is the anode and the pure metal is deposited on the cathode), zone refining (very pure Si, Ge) and vapour phase refining (Ni by the Mond process, Ti by the van Arkel method).","The extraction of iron in the blast furnace: iron ore (Fe₂O₃), coke and limestone are fed in at the top, and hot air is blown in at the bottom. Coke burns to CO. Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂ at about 700 K in the upper part. Limestone gives CaO, which combines with silica to form calcium silicate slag, floating on the molten iron. The product is pig iron, with about 4% carbon, which is converted into steel."],"e":"Copper from its sulphide ore: concentrate by froth flotation, roast in air, smelt with silica to remove iron as slag, and refine electrolytically."},"8.10":{"s":"In f-block elements the electrons fill 4f orbitals (lanthanoids, after lanthanum) or 5f orbitals (actinoids, after actinium). They are shown in two rows below the main table and are called inner transition elements.","x":["In the f-block, the last electron goes into the (n−2)f orbitals. The lanthanoids (cerium to lutetium, Z = 58 to 71, with lanthanum) fill the 4f orbitals, and the actinoids (thorium to lawrencium, Z = 90 to 103, with actinium) fill the 5f orbitals. They are called inner transition elements.","The general configuration of the lanthanoids is [Xe]4f¹⁻¹⁴ 5d⁰⁻¹ 6s², and that of the actinoids is [Rn]5f¹⁻¹⁴ 6d⁰⁻¹ 7s². Some elements have an electron in d (Ce, Gd, Lu), because a half-filled 4f⁷ or filled 4f¹⁴ subshell is stable.","The lanthanoids are often called the rare earths, though they are not rare, apart from promethium, which is radioactive and does not occur naturally in useful amounts. All are silvery, and are mined from minerals such as monazite sand.","The two rows resemble each other in being f-block, but the actinoids differ in several ways, as will be seen: they are all radioactive, they show a wider range of oxidation states, and their 5f orbitals extend further out and take part in bonding."],"e":"Cerium is [Xe]4f¹ 5d¹ 6s². Gadolinium is [Xe]4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s², where the half-filled 4f⁷ subshell is the reason for the electron in 5d."},"8.11":{"s":"Lanthanoids are very alike in chemical properties, because the 4f electrons lie deep inside the atom; this makes them hard to separate, and +3 is their common oxidation state. Actinoids are less alike: they show more oxidation states than the lanthanoids. All f-block elements are metals.","x":["The lanthanoids are very alike in their chemistry, much more so than the d-block elements in a series. The reason is that the electrons are added to the 4f orbitals, which lie deep inside the atom, and the outer electronic structure (5d 6s) and the size of the ions change very little from one element to the next.","That small change is mostly the lanthanoid contraction: the ionic radius falls steadily from La³⁺ (106 pm) to Lu³⁺ (85 pm), because the 4f electrons shield the nuclear charge poorly. It has big consequences: the elements after the lanthanoids (Hf, Ta, W) have nearly the same size as the elements above them (Zr, Nb, Mo), so pairs like Zr–Hf are very difficult to separate, and the basic character of the hydroxides falls from La(OH)₃ to Lu(OH)₃.","The common oxidation state is +3 for all of them. A few show other states in which the f subshell is empty, half-filled or full: Ce⁴⁺ (f⁰), Tb⁴⁺ (f⁷), Eu²⁺ (f⁷) and Yb²⁺ (f¹⁴). Ce⁴⁺ is a good oxidising agent and Eu²⁺ a good reducing agent.","Because the 4f electrons are shielded, they take little part in bonding, and the f–f transitions give sharp, weak absorption bands. Many Ln³⁺ ions are coloured, and many are paramagnetic."],"e":"Zirconium (160 pm) and hafnium (159 pm) have almost the same radius, as a result of the lanthanoid contraction, and are always found together in minerals."},"8.12":{"s":"Lanthanoids are soft, silvery metals. Their common oxidation state is +3, with some +2 and +4. Lanthanoid contraction: atomic and ionic size shrink steadily across the series, because 4f electrons shield the nucleus poorly. As a result, the 4d and 5d elements of the same group (Zr and Hf) are almost the same size.","w":["The lanthanoid contraction is why elements of the second and third transition series (such as Zr and Hf) are nearly the same size."],"x":["Physical properties: lanthanoids are soft, silvery-white metals that tarnish in air. They have high melting points (800 to 1700 °C), which rise slightly across the series, and they conduct well. Their densities are high and rise across the series. They are strong reducing agents, with E° about −2.3 to −2.5 V.","Chemical properties: they are fairly reactive, like calcium. They burn in air to form oxides (Ln₂O₃) and with halogens form halides. They react with water to give hydroxides and hydrogen, slowly in the cold and quickly on heating. They dissolve in dilute acids and release hydrogen. They react with carbon at high temperature to give carbides, and with nitrogen to give nitrides.","The colour of the Ln³⁺ ions is due to f–f transitions. Ions with 0, 7 and 14 f electrons (La³⁺, Gd³⁺, Lu³⁺) are colourless, and the others are pale pink, green, yellow and so on. Most are paramagnetic, except La³⁺ and Lu³⁺, which have f⁰ and f¹⁴.","The basicity of the hydroxides, Ln(OH)₃, falls from La to Lu, because of the decrease in the ionic size, and the ease of separating them by ion exchange chromatography depends on the small differences in the size of the ions."],"e":"Lanthanum hydroxide is a stronger base than lutetium hydroxide, because La³⁺ is larger, and holds the OH⁻ ions less tightly."},"8.13":{"s":"Lanthanoid compounds are present in every household. Inside a colour TV tube or computer monitor, mixed lanthanoid compounds glow in three colours when electrons hit them; europium and yttrium oxide gives the red. Lanthanoids are also used in the Nd:YAG laser, in erbium-doped optical-fibre amplifiers, in hybrid cars, in superconductors and in permanent magnets.","x":["Lanthanoids and their compounds are used widely. Mischmetall, an alloy of about 95% lanthanoids (mainly Ce, La, Nd) with 5% iron and traces of S, C, Ca and Al, is used in lighter flints and in alloy steels, where it removes oxygen and sulphur. Magnesium alloys with lanthanoids are used in aircraft parts as they are light and strong.","Lanthanoid oxides are used in glass, as polishing powder (CeO₂) and in the colouring and decolourising of glass. Phosphors made with europium and yttrium give the red colour in colour television and computer screens, and rare-earth phosphors are used in energy-saving lamps.","Neodymium and samarium make very strong permanent magnets, used in electric motors, headphones, wind turbines and hard disks. Cerium compounds are used in catalytic converters, and in petroleum cracking catalysts. Lanthanoid ions are also used in lasers and in fibre-optic amplifiers (erbium), and in medical imaging (gadolinium contrast agents for MRI)."],"e":"A strong magnet in a mobile phone speaker is usually made of neodymium, iron and boron."},"8.14":{"s":"Actinoids are the 5f series, from thorium to lawrencium (Z = 103). All of them are radioactive. Only thorium and uranium occur in useful amounts in nature; most of the others are made artificially and have short half-lives.","w":["All actinoids are radioactive; the early members (Th, U) are the ones found in nature."],"x":["The actinoids are the 14 elements from thorium (Z = 90) to lawrencium (Z = 103), in which the 5f subshell is filled. They follow actinium (Z = 89). All the actinoids are radioactive. Of them, only thorium and uranium occur in nature in a useful amount, with traces of Pa, Np and Pu. All the elements after uranium are synthetic, made in nuclear reactors or accelerators.","The general configuration is [Rn]5f¹⁻¹⁴ 6d⁰⁻² 7s². The 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals are very close in energy, so the order of filling is irregular, and several elements have an electron in 6d (Th has 6d², Pa 5f² 6d¹, U 5f³ 6d¹).","Like the lanthanoids, the actinoids show a contraction in size across the series (actinoid contraction), but it is larger from element to element, because the 5f electrons shield even less than the 4f ones.","Thorium and uranium are mined from monazite sand and pitchblende (U₃O₈). Uranium is the most important actinoid, as the fuel for nuclear power and weapons."],"e":"Plutonium-239 is not found in nature in any amount. It is made in a reactor by neutron capture in uranium-238, followed by two beta decays."},"8.15":{"s":"Actinoids are silvery, very reactive, radioactive metals. They show more oxidation states than lanthanoids (uranium up to +6), because 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals have similar energies. Actinoid contraction is larger than lanthanoid contraction.","x":["The actinoids are silvery metals, with a high density. They are very reactive, especially when finely divided, and they tarnish quickly in air. They react with boiling water to give oxide and hydride, and dissolve in acids. They are attacked by non-metals at moderate temperatures, but resist alkalis.","The main difference from the lanthanoids is the wide range of oxidation states. The common one is +3, but uranium shows +3, +4, +5 and +6, and neptunium and plutonium up to +7, because the 5f, 6d and 7s energies are close together, so the 5f electrons take part in bonding. The +3 state becomes more stable towards the end of the series.","The ions of the actinoids are coloured, and are paramagnetic, since f–f transitions occur and there are unpaired electrons. The magnetic properties are more complicated than those of the lanthanoids, as the 5f electrons are less shielded.","All isotopes are radioactive. The radiation is a hazard and the elements must be handled in special laboratories. Chemically, the actinoids resemble the lanthanoids at the beginning (Th, Pa, U are close to the d-block elements Hf, Ta, W in some respects) and later behave as true f-block elements."],"e":"Uranium forms the uranyl ion, UO₂²⁺, with uranium in +6, a yellow-green ion that is very stable in solution."},"8.16":{"s":"Uranium is an actinoid that is in great demand because it is used in nuclear reactors. Thorium oxide with 1% cerium oxide was a major source of indoor light in incandescent gas mantles, and there is still a great demand for these lights for outdoor camping, since the oxides turn heat into intense light.","x":["Uranium is the most important actinoid. The isotope U-235 is the fuel of most nuclear reactors and is split by slow neutrons in a chain reaction that releases a huge amount of energy as heat, which is used to make steam and electricity. Natural uranium has only 0.7% U-235, so it is enriched for use in reactors, and more highly enriched for weapons.","Thorium is also a nuclear fuel, as Th-232 can be converted into fissile U-233 in a reactor. India has large deposits of thorium in monazite sands and has a long-term plan to use them. Plutonium-239 is used in weapons and in reactors, and in the power source of some space probes.","Other uses: americium-241 in smoke detectors, in which a tiny amount produces alpha radiation that ionises air, and a drop in the current signals smoke. Thorium oxide was used in gas mantles. Radioactive isotopes are used in medicine for treatment and imaging, and in industry for gauges.","The very long-lived waste from nuclear reactors is a serious problem, and must be stored safely for thousands of years."],"e":"A smoke detector has a few micrograms of americium-241, whose alpha particles ionise the air so that a small current flows. Smoke reduces the current and sets off the alarm."},"8.17":{"s":"Elements beyond uranium (Z > 92) are called transuranium elements and are mostly made artificially in nuclear reactions. Elements 104 to 118 come after the actinoids and are called postactinoid (transactinide) elements. They are extremely unstable, are made only a few atoms at a time, and their names are approved by IUPAC.","x":["The elements with atomic numbers greater than 92 (beyond uranium) are called transuranium elements. They are all radioactive and are made artificially, by bombarding a heavy nucleus with neutrons or with light nuclei in a reactor or accelerator. Neptunium (93) and plutonium (94) were the first, made in 1940.","Those that come after the actinoids (Z greater than 103) are the post-actinoid or transactinoid elements, such as rutherfordium (104), dubnium (105), seaborgium (106), bohrium (107), hassium (108) and meitnerium (109), up to the heaviest known, oganesson (118). They belong to the 6d and 7p blocks.","These nuclei are very unstable, with half-lives from minutes to a fraction of a second, so only a few atoms of some of them have ever been made. Their chemistry is studied one atom at a time. Many are named after scientists: einsteinium (99), fermium (100), mendelevium (101), nobelium (102), lawrencium (103).","The search for the island of stability, a group of superheavy nuclei with a longer life, is one of the aims of current nuclear research."],"e":"Plutonium-239 is made by hitting uranium-238 with neutrons: U-238 + n → U-239, which decays to Np-239 and then to Pu-239 by two beta emissions."}};
